+# Decision: How Concurrent Visit Edits Become a Current Record
+
+## Context
+
+Twenty-four seasonal field workers share 12 Android tablets to survey 320 permanent vegetation plots, producing about 6,000 plot-visit records per season. Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, so updates can arrive days after the starting state was fetched and in any order. Two coordinators review submitted observations and produce a reviewed CSV for a regional science partner every Friday. Spreadsheet imports in the current workflow overwrite corrections without showing which submission was lost.
+
+A visit carries an observation date, an observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some concurrent edits are independent — correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Others are scientifically related — revising a species identification together with its cover estimate. The server cannot reliably infer these relationships from field names. An automatic combination of two related edits can place into the partner's CSV a value that no coordinator ever reviewed as a whole visit.
+
+The HLD assigns the concurrency mechanism to record-store, which owns the submission/review contract that tablet-sync and review-export consume. This decision fixes the submission envelope, the shape of current-state retrieval and review work, and which records are eligible for export, so it sizes both dependent designs. Field-aware three-way merge remains a credible alternative; the criteria below are recorded so a future reader can re-run the judgment against new volumes, staffing, or schema stability rather than re-arguing it. Getting the choice wrong either silently joins scientifically related edits or buries two coordinators under more review work than a season allows.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The invariant that no submitted edit may be silently discarded and that every exported value stays traceable to its originating submission holds for every option and is background, not a decision element. No gate eliminates an option in contention; the choice is a genuine weighing of the following criteria.
+
+- **Scientific integrity — risk of an unnoticed combination of related edits** (**major**; derived from the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). How likely is the mechanism to export a value stitched together from scientifically related edits that no human reviewed as one visit?
+- **Review workload at two coordinators** (**major**; derived from *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). The number of review items per season and how many visits reach export readiness before the Friday deadline. A whole-visit comparison is heavier per item than adjudicating a single highlighted overlapping field, so a smaller queue is worth more than its raw item count suggests.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (**moderate**; same tenet). The amount of merge logic and the ongoing cost of keeping rules correct as the visit schema and scientific conventions evolve.
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent** (**moderate**; derived from *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path ... inspectable*). Whether each stored current record is a visit some worker actually authored, rather than a patchwork of fields.
+- **Submission size over intermittent connectivity** (**minor**; derived from *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*). Bytes per edit and how retries behave on a flaky 48-hour-offline link.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit names the revision the worker started from and submits the complete visit as that worker recorded it. When two or more accepted submissions derive from the same base revision, the server keeps each intact as a competing complete revision. Nothing is combined automatically. A coordinator compares the competing whole visits and records one as the current revision; the others remain in submission history. Even edits to unrelated fields become a review item. The mechanism is a revision identifier and a whole payload, so there is no merge rule set to maintain as the schema changes.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A fetches revision `r7`, corrects an observer name, and submits `r8` (base `r7`). Worker B, also from `r7`, adds a missing species and submits `r9` (base `r7`). `r8` and `r9` are competing; a coordinator compares both whole visits and records one as current.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity: **strong** — no automatic combination, so a related pair of edits is never silently joined; every competing visit is reviewed intact before a value reaches the CSV.
+- Review workload: **weak** — even unrelated field edits create a review item, and the queue grows with every concurrent edit; each item asks a coordinator to compare two whole visits.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — almost no merge logic and no rule set that must be revised as the schema evolves.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **strong** — each stored revision is a visit a worker actually submitted, and the resolved current record is one intact submission.
+- Submission size: **partial** — each edit carries the full visit payload; an idempotency key keeps retries from duplicating edits, but each submission is larger than a delta.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger, fully human-reviewed queue for the absence of merge rules and for intact submitted visits.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit supplies its base state and a structured set of field-level changes. When two submissions edit disjoint fields — the common case, such as an observer-name fix and a newly added species — the server combines them automatically in one round trip, so the visit needs no human review at all and is immediately export-ready. Only overlapping changes create a review item. Field-level provenance records the origin of every value, and the coordinator view highlights the overlap. The price is a maintained rule set for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, kept correct as the schema and scientific conventions change.
+
+**Demonstration.** The two edits above touch disjoint fields, so the server combines them into one current record with no review item. A later pair that both change the same species' cover estimate produces an overlap review item showing both values and their provenance.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity: **weak** — automatic combination can cross relationships the rules do not model, for example a revised species identification and its cover estimate submitted by different workers; provenance records origins but a coordinator adjudicating only an overlap may not see the joined result as a whole visit.
+- Review workload: **strong** — disjoint edits auto-combine, only overlaps are reviewed, and most concurrent visits reach export readiness without coordinator action.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries must be authored and kept correct; a wrong rule silently corrupts combinations until it is noticed.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **partial** — a combined current record can be a patchwork no worker authored as a unit, though each field retains provenance.
+- Submission size: **strong** — structured changes are small and a retry resends only the delta.
+
+**Summary.** Trades maintained merge rules and provenance machinery for a small review queue and earlier export readiness.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.** The decisive criterion is the major one — scientific integrity. The corrections that matter most to the science partner are exactly the scientifically related ones, and no field-level rule set can be trusted to notice when two edits belong together. Whole-visit revisions make the risk impossible by construction: every competing visit is reviewed intact, and related edits are never joined without a coordinator seeing them. The project weighs a larger, fully human-reviewed queue as an acceptable cost against merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits unnoticed. The decision turns on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*. It is taken partly against *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: a bigger queue is real coordinator work, but it is ordinary review rather than specialist maintenance of merge rules, and review already belongs to the two coordinators.
+
+Field-aware three-way merge is not eliminated on principle. It is the option to reconsider if the review queue becomes the binding constraint — if two coordinators cannot clear unresolved visits before the Friday export — or if the visit schema stabilizes enough that maintained rules become cheap and every combined record can be held for whole-visit manual approval. Its advantages on the two major criteria are recorded above so that judgment can be re-run against new volumes or staffing.
+
+**Foreclosed.** Automatic combination of disjoint edits, smaller per-edit submissions, and a smaller review queue. Recovering any of them means revisiting this decision.
+
+**Obligated downstream.** Tablet-sync and review-export consume the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation defined in record-store, and link back to it rather than defining their own concurrency semantics. Specifically:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID with each local draft and keeps it across the 48-hour offline window; submits whole-visit payloads against that base rather than field deltas; builds its retry state machine around the idempotency key plus base revision ID; displays pending competing revisions; and explains server outcomes to field workers, such as a correction becoming one of several competing revisions awaiting review.
+- **Review-export** presents competing complete revisions side by side for coordinator comparison; holds unresolved visits out of the reviewed CSV; sets export eligibility so only resolved current revisions enter the Friday export; and surfaces contributing submissions and authors so the resolved value's provenance stays inspectable. A larger review queue is the accepted consequence of this choice.
+
+Both designs are sized around this contract and are documented separately.
