+## Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission carries the full visit as the worker recorded it, the base revision ID the worker started from, an idempotency key, the author ID, and a client observation timestamp. The server assigns each accepted submission a revision ID and preserves the original payload verbatim.
+
+A revision's base revision ID identifies the current revision the worker had fetched. When two or more accepted submissions name the same base revision, they are competing revisions of that visit, and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator records a resolution. Competing revisions are never combined by the server. An edit whose base has already been superseded by a resolved current revision is still accepted and preserved as a competing revision, so no submitted edit is dropped.
+
+Current-state retrieval returns either the visit's resolved current revision or, for an unresolved visit, the unresolved-work representation: the set of competing revision IDs with their full payloads, authors, and receipt times. A resolution operation records the chosen revision as the current revision together with the resolving coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs. Superseded revisions remain available through submission-history retrieval.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from the resolved current representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse of a key with a different payload is rejected.
+
+Current-state retrieval distinguishes a usable current revision from unresolved review work. Review-outcome operations require coordinator authorization.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
